=========================================================================
0. METHOD — the real components, not a hand-copied repro
=========================================================================

No /admin session is available to any agent, so nothing here is a live
capture. Unlike the T-012/T-004 passes (markup hand-translated into static
HTML), this pass ran the ACTUAL code in headless Chrome:

  - ReportsClient, CareersClient, WhoWeAreClient, the dashboard layout,
    SettingsForm, ImageUploadField, site-content and upload-limits were
    transpiled from the working tree with the project's own TypeScript
    (transpileModule, react-jsx) and run on React 19.2.4's production CJS
    builds and lucide-react 0.575.0 from node_modules.
  - Stubbed at the network edge only: the four "use server" modules (each
    action returns a controllable {ok:true} / {ok:false, error} / throw,
    and can be held in flight), next/link (plain <a>), BrandLogo (a
    same-size box instead of next/image).
  - CSS: the project's tailwind.config.ts + src/app/globals.css compiled with
    the installed Tailwind 3.4.19 CLI (content globs from the project root,
    as in `next build`). Fonts: the self-hosted Inter / Playfair Display
    woff2 files from .next/static/media plus next/font's metric-matched
    fallbacks, so text wraps as in the app.
  - Driven over raw CDP (Node's built-in WebSocket; no Playwright or
    Puppeteer), Chrome launched with --force-color-profile=srgb so sampled
    pixels equal CSS colors. Clicks, Enter/Tab/Shift+Tab/Escape key events,
    real file-input uploads (DOM.setFileInputFiles), typing (insertText),
    hover (mouseMoved).
  - Two builds, same scenarios: BEFORE = /lead-dev's three files (snapshot,
    sha256 27fc7aed / a68b5f94 / 550a5126, i.e. /cso's reviewed baseline);
    AFTER = this pass's files.
  - No server started, no Supabase request, nothing written to the project
    except this folder. Harness source: ./harness/build.mjs, ./harness/run.mjs.
    Raw numbers: ./measurements-before.json, ./measurements-after.json.

Overflow is measured per element, not from the document: html and body are
`overflow-x: clip` (globals.css), so document scrollWidth equals clientWidth
at every width in BOTH builds even while content is being cut off. "No
horizontal scroll" was always trivially true; the real question is whether
anything is clipped out of reach.

